{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak… and you shall say.</b> One needs to know why He repeated saying <b>speak and you shall say</b>, and with different words. And it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, whose memory is for blessing (Tanchuma, Numbers chapter 15), and this is their language: Why is the section of the menorah juxtaposed to the section of the princes? Because when Aaron saw the dedication of the princes, his spirits weakened, for he was not with them in the dedication — neither he nor his tribe. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'By your life, yours is greater than theirs, for you kindle and prepare the lamps every evening and morning,' etc. — end quote. Behold, their words, whose memory is for blessing, require explanation: what comfort does this provide for the weakness of Aaron's spirits over the dedication of the princes, in which he was not included? Is not the service of the menorah the equivalent of a dedication? Moreover, why did He not set his mind at rest with all the offerings that he brings — the daily offerings, the additional offerings, and the incense? Also, in the very offering of the dedication itself by all the princes, he is the one who offers them — so why did He comfort him only with the menorah?" "And the matter may be resolved by giving attention to what they said in Menachot in the chapter Shtei Midot (Menachot 88b), and this is their language: 'How does he do it? He removes them and sets them in the tent and wipes them with a sponge and puts oil in them,' etc. — end quote. And according to the opinion of the one who disagrees with this reasoning and maintains that the lamps are fixed [in the menorah], he also maintains that the tubes into which the lamps were set were thin, and he would bend them downward so that he could prepare and wipe them and then restore them upright as before, as is inferred there from the words of Rashi on the passage beginning 'the prongs,' etc." "<b>But I</b> say: had I been there, I would have proven and ruled before them from the verses that the lamps are not fixed. The first proof: from the fact that when Scripture mentioned every detail of the making of the menorah, it did not mention this detail, that he made 'לְמוּל פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה יָאִירוּ' — for had the lamps been fixed, when it mentioned the making the memory of this matter would have been included. And yet another proof: from what the verse states in the section BaMidbar (Numbers 4:9): 'וְלָקְחוּ בֶּגֶד תְּכֵלֶת וְכִסּוּ אֶת מְנֹרַת הַמָּאוֹר וְאֶת נֵרֹתֶיהָ וְאֶת מַלְקָחֶיהָ וְאֶת מַחְתֹּתֶיהָ וְאֵת כָּל כְּלֵי שַׁמְנָהּ' — this points with a finger that the lamps are not fixed, for if they were, the lamps would be included within the menorah and there would have been no need to specify them. Rather, they are certainly not fixed, and therefore it was necessary to specify them just as it specifies 'its tongs' and 'its fire-pans,' which are objects in their own right." "<b>Furthermore,</b> I saw the like in Divrei HaYamim (II Chronicles 4:20): 'וְאֶת הַמְּנוֹרוֹת וְנֵרוֹתֵיהֶם' — this declares that when mentioning menorahs their lamps are not included. And I also saw Rashi, whose memory is for blessing, there, who wrote, and this is his language: 'To remove from the heart of the commentators [the notion] that the lamps were made of the body of the menorah and attached to it, for behold it is written מְנוֹרוֹת וְנֵרוֹתֵיהֶם,' etc. — end quote; one should examine his words there at length. From this you have learned that the plain sense of the verses declares that the lamps are not fixed." "<b>And according to this</b> we will understand the intent of HaShem's reply to Aaron. For Aaron was sighing over the dedication of the altar, and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: 'By your life,' etc. — meaning, the service of the menorah, in which he takes down the lamps and sets them in the tent and wipes them and then rebuilds it anew and kindles it — behold, every day he performs a new act, thereby dedicating the menorah with each and every kindling. And when they said 'he kindles and prepares them evening and morning,' and we find a kindling only in the evening — behold, they said in tractate Tamid (chapter 3, mishnah 9) that the western lamp must burn day and night, and in Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: 'לְהַעֲלוֹת נֵר' — the western lamp burns continually; should he find it extinguished, he prepares it and kindles it from the altar of burnt offering — end quote. Behold, then, that there is a kindling and a preparing by night and by day, and it follows that he dedicates the menorah every single day, whereas they dedicated the altar only once. And this is what the verse repeated in its utterance <b>speak and you shall say</b>: 'speak' — the speech necessary for the commandment; 'and you shall say' — a soft answer that settles the mind from its weakness, for we said that Aaron's spirits weakened and He answered him that his portion is greater, etc. Furthermore, the word <b>וְאָמַרְתָּ</b> may be explained as an expression of elevation, after the manner of (Deuteronomy 26:17) 'אֶת ה׳ הֶאֱמַרְתָּ' — that He answered him that his portion is elevated above all." "<b>And also</b> according to the view of the one who says they were [merely] fixed lamps — behold, he says that the shafts in which the lamps [sat] were thin, so that one could bend and lower the lamps downward in order to clean them and then raise them back up again, and in the midst of the resting [of the lamps], etc., it would count as renewing [the kindling], and thus he is effectively kindling every day. And on this basis the language of the verse itself is reconciled, as I have written." "<b>When you raise the lamps.</b> One needs to know why it did not say בְּהַדְלִיקְךָ (when you kindle). And our Sages of blessed memory expounded it (Numbers Rabbah 15; Sifrei) with many homiletics. Also one needs to know why HaShem did not command this mitzvah on the first day, for it is a mitzvah that has been practiced from the first day of the [Tabernacle's] service. And furthermore, is not the mitzvah itself already written, for He commands it there in parashat Terumah (Exodus 25:37): ״וְהֶעֱלָה אֶת נֵרֹתֶיהָ וְהֵאִיר עַל עֵבֶר פָּנֶיהָ״ — so why did He repeat it? Further, why did He say <b>they shall give light</b> and not command the one commanded directly: ״you shall kindle,״ parallel to ״when you raise,״ as He also says in parashat Terumah: ״וְהֵאִיר עַל עֵבֶר״ etc.?" "Indeed, with what I prefaced from the statement of Hazal (Menahot 88b), everything resolves properly. For the verse's saying <b>when you raise</b> is because the lamps were not fixed; HaShem commands that at every time he lowers them to clean them and raises them to set them in their place, he must arrange them in such a way that they give light toward the face of the Menorah, etc. And for this reason it also did not say ״you shall kindle,״ because the mitzvah is not at the time of kindling — where it would have been apt to say ״you shall kindle״ — but rather the mitzvah concerns the manner of their placement, that he should set them in an arrangement such that they give light toward [the face of the Menorah], etc. Understand this:" "<b>And with this</b> it is resolved why it was necessary to return to command this, for since until now Moshe had been performing [the service] in this manner during the seven days of inauguration, HaShem was concerned lest Aharon reason in his own mind that the arrangement of their placement is not a disqualifying [requirement], and on the contrary it is a more proper arrangement that the lamps should be straight with their mouths [facing upward] and not diagonally. Therefore HaShem commanded that He tell Aharon that this matter is disqualifying. And He chose to arrange this mitzvah here and did not arrange it together with the arrangements of the offerings that HaShem commanded as service that disqualifies [if altered] — to inform us of Aharon's scrupulousness and his favor, as is recalled in their statement of blessed memory." "<b>Toward the face of the Menorah, etc.</b> The reason for the matter will be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said in tractate Shabbat (22b), and this is their language: ״Outside the curtain of the testimony״ etc. — and does He need its light? And behold, for all forty years that Israel was in the wilderness they walked only by its light! Rather, it is a testimony to the inhabitants of the world that the Shekhinah dwells in Israel. What testimony? Rav said: this is the western lamp, into which one puts oil in the same measure as its fellows, and from it he would kindle and with it he would conclude — end of quote. According to these words, HaShem commands to give light toward the face of the Menorah, which is the western lamp, to show each and every lamp that it exists only for the sake of the testimony that the lamp facing it proclaims — all of them — to demonstrate the miracle mentioned there." "And what I have seen that the Tosafot raised there in their question, ״But does He need its light?״ and so forth — for the reason [that the cloud covered them] all forty years and so forth — and they did not say ״but is not the entire world entirely lit by His light,״ — to the poverty of my mind this is not a precise difficulty. For the question that was tangibly felt by them is more compelling than things that come by faith, since HaShem created sun and moon that illuminate the world, and along this line it will be reconciled with that [teaching] they brought from the end of all the sacrifices and so forth (Menahot 86b); one should examine their words there." "<b>And we have</b> to investigate this from now: that the Menorah came only to declare the miracle that the western lamp would suffice for the lamp on its one side and the lamp on its other side. And it may be said according to the plain sense that it is for beauty — since its introduction was required for the allusion mentioned, it is not fitting to bring into the House of HaShem anything except something properly arranged and correct for lampstands." "And by way of remez (allusion), the seven lamps allude to the nations of the world, which are seventy nations, each one alluding to a grouping of ten. It alludes that all of them illuminate toward the מַעֲרָבִי (western lamp) — they are Israel, which is the aspect of the מַעֲרָב (west), as is known to those who know grace (חֵן). And it also alludes that all of them are consumed, but the lamp of Israel will not be extinguished. And you will find that the illumination of the lamps is only at night, and this is the sod of exile, which is called night — unlike when the time of salvation arrives, which is compared to morning, as it is written (Isaiah 21:12) ״אָתָא בֹקֶר״ and so forth: the lamp of the nations will be extinguished, and there will remain of their totality the lamp of Israel, which goes on growing brighter until the fullness of day. It also alludes in the commandment of kindling the lamps that the one who kindles them should intend at their kindling that their illumination be toward the face of the Menorah — that all things should be for the needs of Israel, which are associated with the Name פנים (face), as is known to those who understand."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:38.956577344Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}